course: dictionary
lesson: 3925
title: Froth
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Froth is a mass of tiny bubbles, usually on top of a liquid. You see it on a milkshake, a frothy coffee or a bubble bath. As a verb, to froth means to make or have froth: a liquid froths when it bubbles up.
explain: «Pour vinegar onto a heap of baking soda and it froths up like a witch's cauldron.» The words froths up tell you the bubbles rise fast, and like a witch's cauldron tells you the mixture looks busy and bubbling. Check your meaning by swapping in bubbles up: the sentence still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Froth has two other meanings you must read carefully for. Something that is froth is light and fun but not very important, like the silly chatter in a show. In informal speech, to froth at the mouth means to be extremely angry. The sentence around the word tells you which meaning is right.
fact: Froth is an uncountable noun, so you say some froth, not a froth.
fact: The verb forms are froth, froths, frothed and frothing.
fact: «An adult froghopper can leap about 70 centimetres up and may be the best high jumper of all for its size.»
fact: «Its young hide in froth they blow from their own sticky liquid, which is not spit.»
fact: In science, froth can mean bubbles of gas trapped in a liquid.
exercise: «Pour vinegar onto a heap of baking soda and it froths up like a witch's cauldron.» What does froths up mean here? | Goes very quiet | Freezes solid | *Bubbles up fast | Turns into a sweet smell
exercise: Mia's milkshake has a thick layer of froth on top. What is the froth? | A cold sweet | *Lots of small bubbles | The glass | A spoon
exercise: «The film was pure froth, with no real story at all.» What does froth mean here? | Something very old | Something very angry | Bubbles in a glass | *Something light and fun but not important
test: «The froghopper's young hide in froth they blow from their own sticky liquid.» Where do the young hide? | Up a tall tree | In the sea | Under a stone | *In froth they blow themselves
test: «Dad was frothing at the mouth when he saw the broken window.» What does frothing at the mouth mean? | Very tired | *Extremely angry | Very hungry | Extremely happy
exam: Which sentence uses froth to mean bubbles? | «The froghopper can leap high.» | «Dad was frothing at the mouth.» | *«Pour vinegar onto baking soda and it froths up.» | «The show was pure froth.»
